From: j7
Subject: j7: ask the gauge on the buses the SoC actually has, at 7-bit addresses

The boot log now reaches the lock screen, so this is the last unverified block on the phone,
and the scan it prints says the scan itself was asking in the wrong places:

    A5XELTE FG: nothing on 0x13830000, stat=0xf0 con=0xe3
    A5XELTE FG: nothing on 0x13840000, stat=0xf0 con=0xe3
    A5XELTE FG: nothing on 0x13850000, stat=0xf0 con=0xe3
    A5XELTE FG: nothing on 0x13860000 (hsi2c), istat=0 ts=0 ctl=0x9
    ...
    A5XELTE FG: nothing on 0x138c0000 (hsi2c), istat=0 ts=0x2000c ctl=0

Two mistakes are visible in those lines.

**The bus table mixes the two I2C blocks this SoC has.**  Per the Exynos 7870 device tree,
`0x13830000`-`0x13890000` are the six S3C2440 controllers (i2c0-i2c5), `0x138a0000`-`0x138c0000`
are hsi2c1-hsi2c3, `0x138d0000`/`0x138e0000` are i2c7/i2c8, `0x138f0000`/`0x13950000`/`0x13960000`
are hsi2c4-hsi2c6, and **hsi2c0 - the controller the PMIC sits on - is `0x10510000`, in the MIF
domain, and was never probed at all.**  The table had 0x13860000-0x13890000 marked as high-speed
controllers and never asked 0x10510000, 0x138f0000, 0x13950000 or 0x13960000, so four of the
seven high-speed controllers and the one bus the bootloader has certainly left running were
missing, and four S3C controllers were driven through the high-speed register map (which is why
they read back the `ctl=0x9` that was written to IICCON).

**The address went out 7-bit on both paths.**  0393 moved the table to 7-bit values (0x3b, 0x36,
0x33) because that is what HSI2C_ADDR wants, but the S3C path still writes the byte straight into
IICDS, which wants the 8-bit form: it has been addressing 0x3b instead of 0x76 since then, on
every S3C controller.  0379 had this right with 0x76 and 0393 lost it.

Both paths now get the form they want: IICDS gets `fg_addr << 1`, HSI2C_ADDR keeps the 7-bit
value in bits 19:10.  The table lists both kinds of controller separately so each bus is driven
through its own register map, and hsi2c0 - the PMIC bus, the one bus whose clock and pinmux the
bootloader proved by using it to bring the phone up - is asked first.  The gauge register map is
unchanged: RSOC 0x08, RVBAT 0x04, RCUR_CC 0x06, RBATCAP 0x0e.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
@@ -125,7 +125,9 @@
 		fg_wr(FG_CON, 0xe3u);
 		fg_wr(FG_FILTER, 7u);
 		fg_wr(FG_STAT, receive ? FG_MODE_RX : FG_MODE_TX);
-		fg_wr(FG_DS, (uint32_t)fg_addr | (receive ? 1u : 0u));
+		/* IICDS takes the 8-bit form of the address: fg_addr is kept 7-bit because
+		 * that is what HSI2C_ADDR wants, so shift it here. */
+		fg_wr(FG_DS, (((uint32_t)fg_addr << 1) & 0xffu) | (receive ? 1u : 0u));
 		delay(1);
 		fg_wr(FG_STAT, (receive ? FG_MODE_RX : FG_MODE_TX) | 0x20u);
 		if (!fg_wait() || (fg_rd(FG_STAT) & 1u)) {
@@ -402,10 +404,17 @@
 	 * Exynos 7870 boards.  Ask every address a fuel gauge can have on every controller,
 	 * the bus Samsung documents first, and keep the first pair that ACKs. */
 	static const struct { uint32_t pa; int hsi2c; } buses[] = {
+		/* hsi2c0, 0x10510000: the PMIC bus, the only controller the bootloader is
+		 * known to have left clocked and pinmuxed, so the gauge is asked here first. */
+		{ 0x10510000u, 1 },
+		/* hsi2c1-hsi2c6. */
+		{ 0x138a0000u, 1 }, { 0x138b0000u, 1 }, { 0x138c0000u, 1 },
+		{ 0x138f0000u, 1 }, { 0x13950000u, 1 }, { 0x13960000u, 1 },
+		/* The S3C2440 controllers: i2c0-i2c5, i2c7, i2c8.  They need their pins
+		 * muxed before a transfer moves, which is the stat=0xf0 in the last log. */
 		{ 0x13830000u, 0 }, { 0x13840000u, 0 }, { 0x13850000u, 0 },
-		{ 0x13860000u, 1 }, { 0x13870000u, 1 }, { 0x13880000u, 1 },
-		{ 0x13890000u, 1 }, { 0x138a0000u, 1 }, { 0x138b0000u, 1 },
-		{ 0x138c0000u, 1 },
+		{ 0x13860000u, 0 }, { 0x13870000u, 0 }, { 0x13880000u, 0 },
+		{ 0x13890000u, 0 }, { 0x138d0000u, 0 }, { 0x138e0000u, 0 },
 	};
 	static const uint8_t addrs[] = { 0x3bu, 0x36u, 0x33u };
 
